        ":question/id": "b0966484-f411-5cf8-8677-c24b7843a43c",
        ":question/problem": "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$\\{5x-2y=5; 2x+y=-1$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-1-2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y & =5 \\\\ 5x-2(-1-2x) & =5 \\\\ 5x+2+4x & =5 \\\\ 9x+2 & =5 \\\\ 9x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=-1-2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1-2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=-\\frac{5}{3}.$"
      },
